/* ==============================================================
   06 — Motion settings for linear moves
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     Meca500 only (R3 or R4)
   TEACHES   SetCartLinVel, SetCartAngVel, SetCartAcc
   NEEDS     Robot activated + homed, no tool mounted
   WATCH     Speed in mm/s — a number the customer already knows
   TIME      ~40 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   REAL UNITS

   Joint moves are tuned in percentages. Cartesian moves are
   tuned in millimetres per second:

       SetCartLinVel(v)    tool tip linear velocity, mm/s
                           (0.001 to 5000; default 150)
       SetCartAngVel(w)    tool reorientation velocity, deg/s
       SetCartAcc(p)       Cartesian acceleration, % of max

   This matters more than it sounds. Process speeds are specified
   in real units: a dispensing spec says 25 mm/s, a wipe test
   says 100 mm/s, an insertion says 5 mm/s. With SetCartLinVel
   you type the number from the spec. There is no calibration
   step and no conversion to get wrong.

   These settings affect ONLY the linear commands — MoveLin,
   MoveLinRelTrf, MoveLinRelWrf. They have no effect on
   MoveJoints or MovePose, which are joint-space moves and obey
   SetJointVel. A customer who sets SetCartLinVel and sees
   nothing change is almost always using MovePose.

   TWO VELOCITIES, ONE MOVE

   A linear move usually translates AND reorients at the same
   time. Those are governed separately: SetCartLinVel for the
   travel, SetCartAngVel for the turning. The robot respects
   both, so whichever is the binding constraint sets the pace.
   If a move seems inexplicably slow, and the tip is turning a
   long way while barely translating, SetCartAngVel is the one
   holding it back.

   THE CEILING STILL APPLIES

   You can ask for 5000 mm/s and not get it. The actual tip
   velocity may be lower — never higher — than requested,
   because the joint velocity limits still apply underneath. Near
   a singularity, a modest tip speed can demand enormous joint
   speed, and the robot will slow the tip to stay within limits
   rather than exceed them.

   A NOTE ON SetCartAcc

   Using SetCartAcc brings the robot to a complete stop between
   moves even when blending is enabled. If you are chasing cycle
   time with blending (intermediate/06), be aware that this
   command works against it.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode — so this
   program starts from firmware defaults no matter what ran
   before it. Reactivating brings the motors back.

   This does NOT move the arm: a robot that was already homed
   needs no re-homing. (With an MEGP 25* gripper fitted, the
   gripper re-homes on reactivation. The robot still does not
   move — but keep the fingers clear.)

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision and calibration config all
   survive. Run common/cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
Home()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(20)

// --- Get into position ----------------------------------------
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
MovePose(190, -70, 280, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(1)


// --- Process speed: 10 mm/s -----------------------------------
// Insertion, probing, delicate placement. Deliberate and slow.
SetCartLinVel(10)
MoveLin(190, 70, 280, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLin(190, -70, 280, 0, 90, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- Process speed: 100 mm/s ----------------------------------
// A typical dispensing or inspection pass. Same two lines.
SetCartLinVel(100)
MoveLin(190, 70, 280, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLin(190, -70, 280, 0, 90, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- Transfer speed: 500 mm/s ---------------------------------
// Moving between stations, where the line is required but the
// speed is not part of the process.
SetCartLinVel(500)
MoveLin(190, 70, 280, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLin(190, -70, 280, 0, 90, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- Translating and reorienting together ---------------------
// Same 140 mm of travel, but the tool also turns 60 degrees
// along the way. Watch how the angular limit governs the pace.
SetCartLinVel(200)
SetCartAngVel(15)                       // deliberately restrictive
MoveLin(190, 70, 280, 0, 90, 60)

// Now allow the reorientation to keep up. Same move, same linear
// velocity, and it completes far faster.
SetCartAngVel(180)
MoveLin(190, -70, 280, 0, 90, 0)


// --- Back to defaults and home --------------------------------
SetCartLinVel(150)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Ask what speed their process runs at, then type it into
      SetCartLinVel and run the pass. Watching their own number
      execute is more convincing than any datasheet figure.

   2. Set SetCartLinVel(1000) and run a pass. It will not be ten
      times faster than the 100 mm/s pass over this short stroke
      — acceleration and the joint limits are governing, not
      your requested velocity. An honest demo of this builds more
      credibility than avoiding it.

   NEXT   07_delay_and_sequencing.mxprog — how the queue really
          executes, and how to synchronise with the outside world.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
